# OS and editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.log

# Netlify / test artifacts
.netlify/
.playwright-mcp/
*-preview.png

# Raw harvests, not needed for deploy (index.html loads the generated data/pl_data.js)
data/*.json
# ...except the frozen epldata referee baseline, which is an INPUT that
# data/extend_ref_history.py needs at runtime (including in the refresh
# Action), not a raw harvest. Must stay committed.
!data/ref_history_base.json
# ...and except the Championship referee set. pl_refs.json is an intermediate
# because pl_data.js is the shipped artifact; the Championship has no data file
# yet, so this JSON is the artifact. Reclassify it as an intermediate (drop
# this line) once eflc_data.js exists and carries a REFS block.
!data/eflc_refs.json
# ...and the fixture list, which the Championship page loads with a script
# tag. Generated, but shipped — not a raw harvest.
!data/eflc_fixtures.js
# ...and La Liga's referee set, for the same reason as the Championship's.
!data/laliga_refs.json
# ...and the published-appointments overlay, which is a HAND-ENTERED SOURCE,
# not a harvest at all — the officials the competition publishes as prose and
# no feed carries in time. It is the only copy: data/eflc_fixtures.js is
# rewritten from API-Football three times a day and re-reads this file every
# time, so an uncommitted overlay is not a missing convenience, it is a
# division that silently reverts to pricing every fixture at a neutral referee
# on the next scheduled run.
!data/appointments.json
# ...and the TRANSFER OVERLAY, for exactly the same reason one step earlier in
# the pipeline. data/pl_data.js is rebuilt from the harvest three times a day,
# so a player removed by hand is back within eight hours; this file is the only
# record of a move the free feed has not published yet. Uncommitted, it is not
# a lost convenience but a squad list that silently reverts.
!data/transfers.json
# ...and La Liga's CLUB REGISTRY, which is not an intermediate at all: it is
# how that division is defined. Spain's twenty are discovered from the feed
# rather than declared in leagues.py, so this file is the only record of which
# clubs the desk is about, and every later stage reads it. Losing it would not
# degrade the build, it would stop it.
!data/laliga_clubs.json
# ...and the Spanish harvest log. `*.log` above is an editor/OS rule; this one
# is a deliberate artifact. The La Liga harvest steps are continue-on-error so
# a Spanish outage cannot take the Premier League refresh down, and twice a
# step went green having done nothing — the only record being a log pane
# somebody had to open. Committing it makes the failure readable from the repo.
!data/laliga_harvest.log
data/epldata/
__pycache__/

# Training tables, not shipped data: the raw per-fixture harvest and the
# leakage-free tables built from it. Tens of thousands of rows apiece,
# regenerable from one command, and read only by build-model.mjs on a runner —
# committing them would put a training set in every visitor's git clone for no
# benefit.
#
# The unprefixed name is listed SEPARATELY on purpose: data/match_history.json
# is the FPL-derived Premier League table, and `data/*_match_history.json` does
# not match it. Its docstring has always said "gitignored" and it never was.
data/match_history.json
data/*_player_matches.json
data/*_match_history.json
!data/player_matches_status.txt
!data/fit_report.txt
# ...and the HAND-SEEDED PENALTY COLUMN, which is a source and not an output.
# No free feed carries penalties per referee, so data/build_refs.py has always
# written pen:null; these figures are read off published fixture graphics and
# typed in, with the source and read-date beside each. data/pl_data.js is
# rebuilt from the harvest, and build_refs re-reads this file on every run — so
# an uncommitted seed is not a lost convenience, it is a column that silently
# empties again on the next scheduled build. It is also the only record of
# WHERE each figure came from, which is the whole basis for admitting
# third-party data at all.
!data/ref_pens.json
# ...and the RECORDED ENDPOINT SHAPES. data/harvest_extra.py was written
# without access to the API — no key, no network — so every parser in it is an
# assumption about a payload nobody had seen. `--probe` fetches one response
# per endpoint and saves it here, and these files are the ONLY evidence in the
# repository of what those endpoints actually return. A shape nobody can look
# at is a shape nobody can check a parser against.
!data/probes/
# ...and the transfer PROPOSALS, which are read by a person rather than
# applied. data/transfers.json stays the hand-curated overlay; this is what the
# feed thinks happened, and the difference is the whole point of keeping both.
!data/*_transfers_feed.json
